 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What the present tense is",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "The present tense says what is happening now, or what happens regularly. In Latin it is one word: the stem of the verb with a person ending on it."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">porta</u><u class=\"v\">mus</u>.",
      "en": "<b>We carry</b>, or we are carrying.",
      "note": "<em>porta-</em> is the stem, \"carry\". <em>-mus</em> is \"we\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "Every verb in Chapter 1 belongs to the <b>1st conjugation</b>: its stem ends in <em>-a</em>.",
      "The stem stays the same; only the ending changes.",
      "The one exception is \"I\": the <em>-o</em> takes the place of the stem's <em>a</em>, so <em>porta-</em> + <em>o</em> is <em>porto</em>."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
