  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Three ways to say it in English",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "English has three present tenses where Latin has one. All three are correct translations; use the one that sounds natural."
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "Simple",
       "Continuous",
       "With \"do\""
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>pugnant</em>",
        "they fight",
        "they are fighting",
        "they do fight"
       ],
       [
        "<em>voco</em>",
        "I call",
        "I am calling",
        "I do call"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">neca</u><u class=\"v\">t</u>.",
      "en": "<b>He kills</b>, or he is killing.",
      "note": "\"Kills\" needs its <em>s</em> in English after \"he\" or \"she\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "The \"do\" form is rare in a straight translation. It comes into its own with \"not\", which is in lesson 1.4."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Make the English agree: \"he carries\", not \"he carry\".",
      "<em>-t</em> is \"he\", \"she\" or \"it\". Any of them is right on its own; the mark is for one person.",
      "<em>amo</em> can be \"I love\" or \"I like\". Both are on the GCSE list."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
